\begin{abstract}
A small grayscale image can be carried inside generated text, and a short message can be carried inside an image. The practical challenge is to recover the represented content from the file actually delivered, rather than from information retained by the sender. We implement a common authenticated recovery protocol for language and pixel autoregressive models and compare three coding rules under fixed carrier budgets. Receivers reconstruct standardized grayscale pixels or literal text bytes from saved UTF-8 and PNG files using agreed settings and a key. Across 120 prospective outcomes, fixed and entropy-gated coding recover every allocated payload in both directions. The finite-message arithmetic implementation recovers all PNG payloads but none of the text payloads before the text-length limit. Successful recovery does not imply concealment: the tested scores completely separate fixed and gated text from ordinary samples, while generated-PNG detection varies with coding rule and observer context. A separate development benchmark achieves a 5.13-fold latency reduction with checked output equivalence. An extension that changes selected photograph pixels also recovers its messages, but a simple parity baseline has slightly better mean fidelity and substantially lower cost. These results distinguish encryption of content from hiding its presence and show that capacity, reproducible decoding, detectability and efficient execution require separate evaluation.
\keywords{Generative steganography \and Large language models \and Image steganography \and Exact recovery \and Steganalysis.}
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
As a hypothetical illustration, a sender encodes a miniature grayscale image into a generated paragraph. The recipient saves the paragraph and reconstructs its pixels using agreed model settings and a key. In the reverse direction, a delivered PNG carries a short text message. The hidden content is the \emph{payload}; the delivered text or image is the \emph{carrier}. Encryption protects contents, whereas steganography concerns carrying them within another medium without revealing their presence.

Our question is: \emph{how reliably and efficiently can information be recovered across generated text and image carriers, and what does that recovery tell us about detectability?} Generative models offer alternative text or pixel choices, but the receiver must reproduce their information mapping from the delivered file. Sender-only token identifiers, cached ranks and intermediate packets cannot establish recovery through that interface.

We integrate existing coding ideas into a common saved-file protocol and compare fixed-rank, entropy-gated and finite-message arithmetic coding on paired payloads. The contribution is the shared protocol and controlled evaluation of recovery, detection and cost. Separate observer-context, GPU and photograph extensions do not enlarge the prospective sample. Exact image recovery means equality of standardized $16\times16$ grayscale pixels, not restoration of an arbitrary original image file. Successful decoding establishes neither naturalness nor concealment.

\section{Related Work}
Calgacus transfers context-dependent token ranks and discusses cross-domain use~\cite{calgacus}. A \emph{rank} is a symbol's position in the model's ordered choices. RankCloak develops bounded byte coding, entropy gating and replay practices, and reports rendered-text recovery difficulties~\cite{rankcloak}. We inherit these foundations rather than claim rank transfer or cross-domain transport as new.

Neural Linguistic Steganography uses arithmetic decoding to generate text and encoding to recover bits~\cite{ziegler}; Pixel-Stega applies arithmetic stegosampling to autoregressive images~\cite{pixelstega}. Our independently implemented comparator has explicit finite-message framing. Published stepwise verification addresses tokenization inconsistency~\cite{consistency}; we adapt complete-prefix verification. GLTR provides precedent for probability and rank scores~\cite{gltr}, whereas our controls are ordinary samples from the same eligible model distribution.

Image embedding precedents include quantization index modulation, syndrome-trellis codes, HiDDeN and StegaStamp~\cite{qim,stc,hidden,stegastamp}. Our photograph extension reconstructs model partitions from coarse pixels and compares with simple parity. CUDA graphs are established infrastructure~\cite{pytorchgraphs}; we test acceleration while preserving decoding behavior.

\section{Recovery from Delivered Files}
\subsection{Shared Protocol and Receiver Boundary}
The sender seals source bytes into an encrypted and authenticated packet and maps its information to model choices. The receiver obtains only the saved carrier, profile, shared context and key. The profile fixes models, tokenizer, numerical rules, coder and budget. Source bytes/digests, sender token IDs, ranks and packets are excluded. After the sender exits, a fresh receiver reconstructs and authenticates the packet; a separate evaluator checks source equality (Fig.~\ref{fig:overview}).

