Image payloads are 256 unsigned bytes: row-major $16\times16$ grayscale pixels. Text payloads are literal UTF-8 without normalization, with a declared range of 32--128 bytes. An eight-byte header identifies version, type, source length and dimensions; a 256-byte source slot is zero padded. AES-256-GCM~\cite{gcm} encrypts and authenticates these 264 bytes with protocol-bound associated data. A 12-byte nonce and 16-byte tag make a fixed 292-byte, or 2,336-bit, carried packet. Keys and nonces use operating-system randomness; paired coders reuse sealed ciphertext without re-encryption. Parsing validates type, dimensions, length, UTF-8 and padding after authentication. Failure returns no plaintext. This authenticates content, not concealment.

\subsection{Observable Choices and Serialization}
At step $t$, eligible symbols $E_t$ have normalized probabilities $q_t(v)$. Text ranks sort descending logits, image ranks descending log probability; ascending identifiers break ties. Float64 probability bookkeeping rejects invalid distributions and excludes numerical zero mass without adding a floor. Ordinary sampling uses temperature one and inverse CDFs in identifier order.

The text model is Llama 3 8B Instruct, Q4\_K\_M GGUF~\cite{llama3}. Its top 256 tokens are selected \emph{before} filtering out special/control tokens, empty renderings and invalid UTF-8. Let $s$ be the carrier's token prefix, $D$ exact byte rendering without cleanup, and $T$ tokenization without special-token interpretation. Each candidate $v$ must satisfy
\begin{equation}
T(D(s\mathbin{\|}[v]))=s\mathbin{\|}[v].
\label{eq:serialization}
\end{equation}
Individually valid tokens can merge or split when rendered text is retokenized. The model sees one beginning-of-sequence token, a separately tokenized prompt, then the carrier, without a chat template. The receiver tokenizes the saved carrier separately and verifies byte equality. Payload choices, skipped positions and ordinary completion all obey Eq.~\ref{eq:serialization}.

PixelCNN++~\cite{pixelcnn}, pretrained on CIFAR-10, models a $32\times32$ RGB canvas. A separately shared $1\times32$ conditioning row occupies its first row. The delivered $32\times31$ RGB8 PNG has 992 pixels, or 2,976 channel values, read without color conversion. The receiver restores the row internally and replays raster/R--G--B order. One forward per visible pixel supplies ten discretized logistic mixture components. CDF differences give channel masses with boundary tails. Green conditions on observed red; blue on red and green. Updating mixture weights after each observation is essential: independent channel marginals would change the distribution.

\subsection{Coding, Capacity and Stopping}
\paragraph{Fixed rank.}
Each packet byte is split into high and low four-bit groups. Value $d\in\{0,\ldots,15\}$ chooses rank $d+1$, so 584 positions carry the packet. The receiver inverts those ranks. Fewer than 16 eligible symbols while bits remain causes a support failure.

\paragraph{Entropy-gated rank.}
\emph{Entropy gating} uses fixed-rank coding only when the model offers enough uncertainty, measured by
\begin{equation}
H(q_t)=-\sum_{v\in E_t}q_t(v)\log_2q_t(v)>\tau.
\end{equation}
Otherwise an ordinary sample consumes a carrier position without payload bits. Both endpoints recompute the gate. Development fixed $\tau\approx0.598397$ bits for text and $3.215951$ bits for channels; full-precision thresholds remain unchanged.

\paragraph{Arithmetic A1.}
Following~\cite{ziegler}, A1 begins with $[0,2^{32})$ and partitions the current interval according to $q_t$. At width $R$, it retains symbols with $q_t(v)\geq1/R$ and at least the highest-probability $\min(2,|E_t|,R)$ symbols. Renormalized widths are rounded to nearest integer, ties to even; bins stop before cumulative overflow, residual width goes to the first symbol, and zero-width bins are removed. The next 32 packet bits select a bin, with zero lookahead only beyond the packet. Both endpoints emit common leading bits of the lower endpoint and upper endpoint minus one, then shift the interval. A step can consume a symbol without emitting bits.

Completion is the first emission reaching 2,336 bits; up to 31 excess bits must be zero and are discarded. There is no implicit flush or endpoint dump. This differs from the reference's final-token handling. A1 can stagnate when an unchanged narrow interval straddles a leading-bit boundary; observed failures therefore require trace-based diagnosis.

Text has a 2,048-token cap and requires 32 ordinary tokens after packet completion. A packet finishing too late for this tail is not a conforming carrier. PNG generation fills all 2,976 channels after packet completion; neither metadata nor appended bytes carry payload. Inversion follows inductively when observed prefixes reproduce identical distributions and transitions. Serialization, sufficient support and capacity are separate necessary conditions.

\subsection{Extension to Existing Photographs}
The photograph profile carries the same packet through a $256\times256$ RGB image. For each channel, preserve $B(c)=4\lfloor c/4\rfloor$. Both endpoints can then reconstruct the coarse context $B+2$ from delivered pixels, without the original cover. PixelCNN++ evaluates 64 fixed $32\times32$ tiles. At each selected pixel, the 64 colors in its $4\times4\times4$ cell are ranked by joint conditional log probability, with lexicographic ties. Zero-based rank parity forms two equal bit classes. The sender selects the nearest color by squared RGB distance in the required class; the receiver reads its rank parity.

Domain-separated HKDF/HMAC-SHA256 placement, derived from the key, coarse-image hash and agreed protocol, selects 2,336 distinct pixel positions. It does not depend on the hidden packet nonce. The baseline uses $(r+g+b)\bmod2$ with identical packet, positions and nearest-distance rule. Channel changes are bounded by three. These coarse-context ranks differ from generated-PNG likelihoods. The receiver needs PNG, profile and key; unused pixels are not authenticated.

\section{Experimental Design}
\subsection{Sources, Pairing and Uncertainty}
The prospective allocation contains 20 held-out image payloads and 20 held-out text payloads, each evaluated with all three coders: $20\times2\times3=120$ outcomes. Images are Fashion-MNIST test samples, two per class, BOX-resized from $28\times28$ to $16\times16$~\cite{fashion}. Texts are nonoverlapping spans from Austen's \emph{Pride and Prejudice} and Doyle's \emph{The Adventures of Sherlock Holmes}, from frozen Gutenberg sources: ten each in the 32--64 and 65--128-byte bands, with realized lengths 58--128 bytes and mean 93.2. Payload sources were disjoint from development; sources, contexts and thresholds were frozen before generation. Development contexts were reused. The text prompt requests a calm forest field journal; the PNG row comes from an independent development image.

One sealed packet is shared by the three methods within each payload/context group. Failures are retained. Each group owns an ordinary control: a 2,048-token text trace with length-matched prefixes, or a full PNG. These are \emph{40 independent controls}; reuse does not create observations.

\emph{Goodput} counts useful source bits actually delivered. For $L$ source bytes and $N$ carrier symbols, $G=8L/N$ for authenticated, exact, conforming recovery, and zero otherwise. Text uses bits/token and PNG bits/channel; the latter multiplied by three gives bits/pixel. Padding, framing and completion consume capacity without increasing useful bits. Recovery intervals are two-sided 95\% Clopper--Pearson bounds under a Bernoulli working model. Continuous quantities and AUCs use the retained 2,000 stratified group-bootstrap draws, keeping paired methods and owned controls together within image class or text-length band. Percentile intervals describe this fixed allocation. Boundary paired-binary comparisons also retain conservative marginal bounds; collapsed bootstrap intervals are not certainty.

\subsection{Observer Knowledge and Execution Cost}
The observer knows the model, eligibility rules and initially the correct context, but neither key nor source. Frozen scores are mean surprisal and log-rank:
\begin{equation}
S(y;c)=-\frac{1}{N}\sum_t\log_2q_t(y_t),\qquad
R(y;c)=\frac{1}{N}\sum_t\log_2\rank_t(y_t).
\end{equation}
Higher scores always indicate steganography, without post-hoc reversal. Area under the ROC curve (AUC) compares steganographic and ordinary scores with half credit for ties. Scorable failures remain included. No detector training or human assessment is involved.

A later context comparison reuses the same 60 generated PNGs and 20 controls. It substitutes a previously frozen random-RGB row for the correct row while keeping the exact model known. Paired changes jointly resample observer conditions, methods and shared controls in the same 20 groups. These are repeated scores, not new transmissions.

Original inference ran serially on an NVIDIA RTX 5000 Ada with 32 GB memory. Text uses llama-cpp-python 0.3.23 with all 33 eligible layers offloaded; images use PyTorch 2.5.1+cu124, float32 inference and float64 probability calculations. Deterministic settings, disabled TF32/mixed precision and fixed model identities support reproducible ranks. Reported charged latency spans sender and receiver launch to exit, including imports, loading, occupied CPU work, serialization and teardown. The independent evaluator is outside this boundary; nested phase and CUDA times are not added again.

The separate GPU benchmark matches reference and CUDA-graph execution on three development messages (32, 96 and 128 bytes). Three fixed-rank repetitions per message yield nine timing pairs; one gated and one A1 comparison yield eleven comparisons overall. Both modes use fresh processes and identical retained packets. Acceptance requires exact eligible identifiers, rank order, float64 probability streams, PNG bytes and recovered bytes. The separate photograph study uses six development and twenty test BSDS500 covers, selected by lowest numeric identifier, converted to RGB and center-cropped without resizing~\cite{bsds}. Arms share texts and positions; comparisons are descriptive paired summaries.

\section{Results}
\subsection{Recovery, Useful Rate and Finite Capacity}
The 120 outcomes comprise 100 exact, authenticated, conforming recoveries and 20 arithmetic-text failures (Fig.~\ref{fig:recovery}). Fixed and gated coding recover 20/20 in both directions; A1 recovers 20/20 PNG and 0/20 text payloads. Corresponding 95\% recovery bounds are [83.16\%, 100\%] and [0\%, 16.84\%].

