Under the mismatched row, all 80 PNGs remain scorable (Fig.~\ref{fig:context}). Fixed AUC falls to 0.6975, a paired change of $-0.1875$ [$-0.2775$, $-0.1000$]. Gated AUC becomes 0.7475, a change of $-0.0825$ [$-0.1625$, 0.0100], and A1 becomes 0.5925, a change of 0.0350 [$-0.0225$, 0.1050]. Only the fixed reduction is resolved by its paired interval. Primary fixed and gated mismatch intervals still exclude 0.5; the fixed secondary interval includes it. These intervals do not establish a between-method sensitivity difference. Moreover, the correct row comes from generated imagery and the alternate row from random RGB values: context correctness is not isolated from row statistics.

\subsection{Execution Cost and the Photograph Baseline}
CUDA-graph replay yields a 5.133-fold reduction in matched fixed-rank latency across nine timing pairs on three development payloads (Table~\ref{tab:photo}a). All eleven comparisons preserve exact probability/rank streams and artifacts, and all 22 carriers recover correctly. Graph setup is included in charged time. Reserved memory rises from 516 to 744 MiB. This stack-specific development result does not replace the original prospective timings.



Both photograph arms recover 6/6 development and 20/20 held-out messages (Table~\ref{tab:photo}b). PSNR uses RGB mean-square error and peak 255; SSIM averages channels with an $11\times11$ Gaussian ($\sigma=1.5$), valid centers, population covariance and $K_1=.01$, $K_2=.03$. The model arm changes at most two channel levels in the observed sample; parity changes at most one. Its paired mean PSNR is 0.144 dB lower than parity's, with substantially greater runtime. All 46,720 selected learned partitions differ from parity even allowing label swaps, yet distinct partitions confer no demonstrated recovery, fidelity or cost advantage. Fixed-context tile evaluation differs from autoregressive generation, precluding a matched speedup claim. Photograph fidelity establishes neither concealment nor editing resistance; generated-PNG detector results do not transfer here.

\section{Discussion and Limitations}
Saved-file recovery requires reproducible serialization. A separate development comparison recovers 40/40 cases with complete-prefix filtering versus 22/40 with singleton filtering, using twenty payloads under two prompts. The eighteen failures remain unrepaired; forty cases are not forty independent payloads. Lossless PNG resaves were checked, but JPEG conversion, cropping and paraphrasing were not evaluated.

Gating trades capacity and time for lower average surprisal while leaving the tested text scores completely separated. A1's lower PNG separation applies only to the stated observer and sample; incomplete text information cannot count as delivery. Faster image inference cannot supply missing bits or remove text's CPU filtering bottleneck. Photograph fidelity and distinct learned partitions likewise do not establish concealment.

Two pinned models, twenty groups per direction and one alternate context limit generalization. Shared controls and timing repetitions remain dependent. Decoding requires compatible numerical execution, models and keys. Full profiles and evidence remain in the repository; the main claims require no supplementary PDF.\footnote{\url{https://github.com/mantzaris/ImageCalgacus}}

\section{Conclusion}
Generative models support exact cross-medium recovery under a shared protocol, but usefulness depends separately on capacity, reproducible decoding, detectability and cost. Serialization consistency is essential for saved text; insufficient capacity prevents delivery even when endpoints agree. Exact recovery can coexist with easy detection, whose measurement depends on observer assumptions. Acceleration is useful when decoding remains consistent. Learned image constructions also need simple alternatives: here parity recovers the same messages with slightly better mean fidelity and much lower cost.

